# GDScript conventions

## Avoid brittle `res://` paths

- Prefer `class_name` for shared scripts — call by type, no `preload("res://...")`
- Prefer `@export` / `@export_file` / PackedScene refs set in the editor
- Prefer relative `preload("sibling.gd")` only when files must stay colocated
- Hardcoded `res://...` OK only for rare path maps (e.g. HFSM scene id → `.tscn`)

## Naming

- Signals: `ev_*` (`ev_start_game`, `ev_pawn_died`, `ev_charges_changed`)
- Files: `snake_case.gd`; scene colocated (`foo.gd` + `foo.tscn`)
- Types: `PascalCase` + `class_name` when shared
- FSM state IDs: `static func get_state() -> String`

## Patterns

- Wire dependencies with `@export` / `@export_category` (editor-first)
- **UI in the editor**: add/move/style nodes in `.tscn`; code updates state
- Pure logic → `RefCounted` (`CombatResolver`); Nodes stay thin
- Signal subscriptions → `EventListener`; tear down in `deinit()` / `_exit_tree`
- Apply config copies with `ResourceUtils.update_resource()` when overriding live Resources
- Spawn: `PackedScene.instantiate()`; prefer deferred add when colliding with physics setup
- Extensible entities: nested components (see `Pawn.MovementComponent`, `ShieldEntity`, `AttackEntity`)

## Must

- Prefix new cross-object signals with `ev_`
- Tear down listeners on context/FSM leave
- Use existing 3D physics layers: pawn=1, attack=2, shield=3, barrier=4, abyss=5, world=6
- Use InputMap actions (`attack`, `shield`) + touch joystick — support Mobile + desktop
- New controls/UI must be usable with fingers on landscape phone

## Layers

- Feature code stays inside `src/features/{name}/`; expose signals/`setup`, not cross-feature internals
- `core/` code must not reference `src/` or this-game types
- Scenes orchestrate; they don't absorb feature implementation

## Scene isolation

- Every `.tscn` must run isolated (editor F6) without crashes
- Required data lives in exported `.tres` defaults on the scene
- Parents override via events → `initialize`
- Do not hard-require parent context, autoloads, or sibling scenes to boot

## Avoid

- Autoloads for new game flow (use `RootEvents` / `GameEvents` Resources)
- Feature A importing Feature B internals (compose in game scene)
- Putting project-specific types into `core/`
- Building full menus/HUD programmatically when a `.tscn` would do
- New parallel FSM unless existing `core/lib/fsm` cannot fit
- Drive-by renames without explicit request
